strip - libc version of /usr/bin/strip (strip.c 1.2 11/19/82)
==============================================================

Functions of the libc part that differ from the shipped libc.a: see ../../lib/oldlibc/CATALOG.md (usr/bin/strip).

strip.spec, strip.link   the libc part of /usr/bin/strip as modules (input of host/tools/unlink.py, result: link
           order); the old modules are `../../lib/oldlibc/<module>/0_<n>.s`, see there. `make` here links `strip`
           from the own objects, these modules and the shipped members that are identical with the original
           (extracted from /lib/libc.a); no libc is linked. 16 of 40 modules are old (fseek rdwr printf doprnt ffltioc
           filbuf flsbuf endopen findiop cnvt length malloc mktemp strcpy goodmagic swap).

The built file is not byte-identical with the original (9078 instead of 9072 bytes; the bss has the size of the original): strip.o of the original was made
by an older cc, its own code differs in two blocks (D2) and its string literals are not padded to even addresses
(D12, 6 bytes of data). The libc part is: `tools/cmpins.py strip /usr/bin/strip 0x1fd2 0x1fd2 0x68c` gives 0 differing
blocks. Run on several files (objects, a program, `-h`, a missing file) the built `strip` gives the same output and
files as the original.

The old modules cnvt, ffltioc, findiop, flsbuf, goodmagic and length are the same text as in size (one version
each); doprnt, filbuf, endopen, malloc, printf, rdwr and swap are different versions (0_2 here).
